## Root-cause notes (for DE / SPRINT T4)
1. **Cap-gate strip scripts (SPRINT names)** — `patch_awk` / `boot_dbg` are a **debris checklist**, not live files. Absence is PASS. Do not invent residual “present” status. Keep `hygiene_t4_smoke` as the absence gate.

2. **8.1 DEBUG** — `tc_control_cond.oo` product `println("ERR\ttype\t...")` is fail-closed diagnostics, not a debug leak. Smoke correctly allows `ERR\ttype` and fails on `DBG_`/`DEBUG`.

3. **Minisign “fingerprint” fiction (pre-OWN)** — comments claimed hardcoded FP was the trust root, but code only refused `TODO_PUBKEY_FP` and logged the string. Real gate was `minisign -V -p <tree pub>`. Tree-rewrite of pub+sig was a full trust forge. **Body pin closes the script-vs-seed-dir gap** under the threat model “attacker rewrites seed assets, not bootstrap scripts.”

4. **gitignore ≠ untrack** — `/bootstrap/seed/oodac` in gitignore while blob remains in HEAD is the classic supply-chain footgun. SIGNING.oot “No (gitignored)” is currently **false**. Closing requires `git rm --cached` + documented CI seed from release asset / `SEED_OODAC` secret — coordinate with CI owner; not a silent untrack this pass.

5. **Host path leak in seed** — rebuild seed in clean path or strip; low crypto impact, high opsec smell for a published trust root.

6. **Bak binaries** — local rebuild workflow (`cp oodac oodac.bak`) is fine; shipping them is not. Ignore patterns + smoke are the control; delete local bak when done.

